# Java 8 Functional Interfaces

A functional interface is an interface that has exactly one abstract method.

## 1. Predicate<T>

Used for testing a condition.

Predicate<Integer> p = n -> n > 10;

System.out.println(p.test(15)); // true

Method: boolean test(T t)

---

## 2. Consumer<T>

Used for consuming/processing a value. Does not return a result.

Consumer<String> c = name -> System.out.println(name);

c.accept("Anil");

Method: void accept(T t)

---

## 3. Supplier<T>

Used for supplying/generating a value. Takes no input.

Supplier<String> s = () -> "Hello";

System.out.println(s.get());

Method: T get()

---

## 4. Function<T, R>

Used to convert one value into another.

Function<String, Integer> f = name -> name.length();

System.out.println(f.apply("Anil")); // 4

Method: R apply(T t)

---

## 5. UnaryOperator<T>

Takes one value and returns the same type.

UnaryOperator<Integer> u = n -> n * 2;

System.out.println(u.apply(5)); // 10

Method: T apply(T t)

---

## 6. BinaryOperator<T>

Takes two values of the same type and returns the same type.

BinaryOperator<Integer> b = (a, c) -> a + c;

System.out.println(b.apply(10, 20)); // 30

Method: T apply(T t1, T t2)

---

## 7. BiPredicate<T, U>

Takes two values and returns boolean.

BiPredicate<Integer, Integer> p = (a, b) -> a > b;

System.out.println(p.test(20, 10)); // true

Method: boolean test(T t, U u)

---

## 8. BiConsumer<T, U>

Takes two values and returns nothing.

BiConsumer<String, Integer> c =
(name, age) -> System.out.println(name + " " + age);

c.accept("Anil", 30);

Method: void accept(T t, U u)

---

## 9. BiFunction<T, U, R>

Takes two values and returns a result.

BiFunction<Integer, Integer, Integer> f =
(a, b) -> a + b;

System.out.println(f.apply(10, 20)); // 30

Method: R apply(T t, U u)

---

# Other Common Functional Interfaces

## Runnable

Takes no input and returns nothing.

Runnable r = () -> System.out.println("Running");

r.run();

Method: void run()

---

## Callable<V>

Takes no input and returns a value; commonly used with concurrency.

Callable<Integer> c = () -> 100;

System.out.println(c.call());

Method: V call() throws Exception

---

# Quick Summary

Interface | Input | Output | Main Method
Predicate<T> | 1 | boolean | test()
Consumer<T> | 1 | nothing | accept()
Supplier<T> | 0 | T | get()
Function<T,R> | 1 | R | apply()
UnaryOperator<T> | 1 | same T | apply()
BinaryOperator<T> | 2 | same T | apply()
BiPredicate<T,U> | 2 | boolean | test()
BiConsumer<T,U> | 2 | nothing | accept()
BiFunction<T,U,R> | 2 | R | apply()
Runnable | 0 | nothing | run()
Callable<V> | 0 | V | call()

# Easy Way to Remember

Predicate → condition → boolean

Consumer → consume → nothing

Supplier → supply → value

Function → convert → value

The Bi... versions generally mean two inputs, while UnaryOperator and BinaryOperator are specialized versions of Function for the same input/output type.
